14. ''Empirical Formulae for the Saturation-Pressure.''—The values of the saturation-pressure have been very accurately determined for the majority of stable substances, and a large number of empirical formulae have been proposed to represent the relation between pressure and temperature. These formulae are important on account of the labour and ingenuity expended in devising the most suitable types, and also as a convenient means of recording the experimental data. In the following list, which contains a few typical examples, the different formulae are arranged to give the logarithm of the saturation-pressure <math>p</math> in terms of the absolute temperature <math>\theta</math>. As originally proposed, many of these formulae were cast in exponential form, but the adoption of the logarithmic method of expression throughout the list serves to show more clearly the relationship between the various types.
{{block center|<math>\log p = \text{A}+\text{B}\theta</math> . . . . (Dalton, 1800). . . (19)}}
{{block center|<math>\log p = \text{C} \log (\text{A}+\text{B}\theta)</math> . . . . (Young, 1820).}}
{{block center|<math>\log p = \text{A}\theta/(\text{B}+\text{C}\theta)</math> . . . . (Roche, 1830).}}
{{block center|<math>\log p = \text{A}+\text{B}b^\theta+\text{C}c^\theta</math> . . . . (Biot, 1844; Regnault).}}
{{block center|<math>\log p = \text{A}+\text{B}/\theta+\text{C}/\theta^2</math> . . . . (Rankine, 1849).}}
{{block center|<math>\log p = \text{A}+\text{B}/\theta+\text{C} \log \theta</math> . . (Kirchhoff, 1858; Rankine, 1866).}}
{{block center|<math>\log p = \text{A}+\text{B}/\theta^b</math> . . . . (Unwin, 1887).}}
{{block center|<math>\log p = \text{A}+\text{B} \log \theta+\text{C} \log (\theta+\text{c})</math>. (Bertrand, 1887).}}
{{block center|<math>\log p = \text{A}+\text{B}/(\theta+\text{C})</math> . . . . (Antoine, 1888).}}

The formula of Dalton would make the pressure increase in geometrical progression for equal increments of temperature. In other

